 The software compositor -- H4b. The guios shell renders its widget
 tree through codex-vm's host rasterizer (a command buffer at MMIO
 0xBE000000 flushed by ports 0x400-0x402), a device no real machine
 has. But the GPU walk only ever emitted axis-aligned filled
 rectangles, and text was always a separate direct-to-framebuffer
 pass -- so the whole pipeline ports to a plain walk of the laid-out
 tree: resolve each node's style from the theme, fill its bounds,
 draw its text, recurse into its children so they paint over their
 parent. Same foreword widget system, same flex layout, same theme
 records; only the final metres of the pipeline change.

 **All seven widget kinds are drawn here as of 2026-08-03.** This handled
 five and answered `otherwise -> 0` for `WkInput` and `WkCustom`, which is
 a widget that lays out, occupies its space, and paints nothing -- the
 shape that reads as a layout bug when it is a missing arm. The two
 arrived when `apps/guios/GopRender.codex` was compared against this one
 during the guios retirement: that chapter drew all seven, against a
 GopBuf through codex-vm's host rasterizer, and is the one being retired.

 We say:

Section: Decoration

 The framebuffer is 32-bit XRGB with no alpha channel and no read-modify-write
 blend anywhere in this path, so a shadow here is a hard offset rectangle and
 a gradient is an exact per-row (or per-column) colour, never a blend against
 what is already there. A shadow therefore paints BEFORE the box it belongs to
 and is overwritten by it.

  comp-chan : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-chan (c) (sh) = bit-and (bit-shru c sh) 255

  comp-lerp : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-lerp (a) (b) (i) (n) =
    if n <= 1 then a
    else let d = n - 1
    in let j = if i > d then d else i
    in let r = (comp-chan a 16 * (d - j) + comp-chan b 16 * j) / d
    in let g = (comp-chan a 8 * (d - j) + comp-chan b 8 * j) / d
    in let bl = (comp-chan a 0 * (d - j) + comp-chan b 0 * j) / d
    in bit-shl r 16 + bit-shl g 8 + bl

  comp-corner-r : CornerStyle -> Integer
  comp-corner-r (c) = when c
    is CornerSharp -> 0
    is CornerRound (r) -> r
    is CornerBevel (r) -> r

 A bevel steps in one pixel per row. A round corner steps in by the horizontal
 half-chord of a circle of radius r whose centre sits r pixels in from the
 corner, so the inset at a row d below the top is r - isqrt(r^2 - (r-d)^2).

 A radius larger than half the box would inset every row and erase the widget,
 so it is clamped to what the box can hold before it is used anywhere.

 Every length in a style is a LOGICAL pixel and every rectangle handed to these
 is a DEVICE rectangle, so a radius, a border width or a shadow offset is
 multiplied by the scale here while a bound arrives already multiplied.

  comp-fit-r : CornerStyle, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-fit-r (c) (w) (h) (s) = math-min (comp-corner-r c * s) (math-min w h / 2)

 The inset a row actually wants is not a whole number of pixels, and rounding
 it to one is what makes a corner a staircase. `comp-corner-ins256` answers the
 same quantity in 256ths: the integer part names the last pixel OUTSIDE the
 shape and the fraction is how much of that pixel the shape misses, so
 `255 - fraction` is its coverage. `math-isqrt (v * 65536)` is `sqrt v * 256`
 to the same one-unit accuracy `math-isqrt` already has.

  comp-corner-ins256 : CornerStyle, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-corner-ins256 (c) (w) (h) (y) (s) =
    let k = comp-fit-r c w h s
    in if k <= 0 then 0
    else let d = math-min y (h - 1 - y)
    in if d >= k then 0
    else when c
      is CornerSharp -> 0
      is CornerBevel (r) -> (k - d) * 256
      is CornerRound (r) -> let dy = k - d in k * 256 - math-isqrt ((k * k - dy * dy) * 65536)

Section: The Clip

 The framebuffer is linear and `gop-put` is one `poke-32` at
 `(y * stride + x) * 4`, so it has no bottom and no right-hand edge: a rectangle
 whose y runs past the last scanline writes past the end of the framebuffer, and
 one whose x runs past the row lands on the NEXT row. `gop-draw-text` bounds x
 against `stride` for that second reason and says so; nothing has ever bounded
 the first.

 Measured 2026-08-20 at 1600x900 with the Browser pane open: rows 900 to 977
 were written past a framebuffer whose last row is 899, 499,200 bytes of it,
 every paint. Nothing clips a node to its box, so a child laid out taller than
 its parent paints the difference over whatever is below and then over whatever
 is after.

 Every rectangle this chapter fills now goes through `comp-fill`, which is
 `gop-fill-rect` intersected with the clip. A caller that hands it a rectangle
 outside the clip gets nothing drawn rather than memory scribbled on, and the
 clip is a DEVICE rectangle like everything else at this level.

  comp-fill : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-fill (base) (stride) (clip) (px) (py) (pw) (ph) (color) =
    let x0 = math-max px (clip.lr-x)
    in let y0 = math-max py (clip.lr-y)
    in let x1 = math-min (px + pw) (clip.lr-x + clip.lr-w)
    in let y1 = math-min (py + ph) (clip.lr-y + clip.lr-h)
    in if x1 <= x0 then 0
    else if y1 <= y0 then 0
    else gop-fill-rect base stride x0 y0 (x1 - x0) (y1 - y0) color

 A gradient row is drawn a pixel at a time so that the interpolation is over the
 box rather than over the visible part of it, which is why this clamps the span
 instead of shifting it: a box half off the bottom keeps the colours it would
 have had.

  comp-row-h : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-row-h (base) (stride) (clip) (bx) (bw) (y) (x) (xend) (a) (b) =
    if x >= xend then 0
    else let p = if x < clip.lr-x then 0
      else if x >= clip.lr-x + clip.lr-w then 0
      else gop-put base stride x y (comp-lerp a b (x - bx) bw)
    in comp-row-h base stride clip bx bw y (x + 1) xend a b

 A row with no corner takes the old path exactly: one span, no reads. Only the
 rows a corner actually cuts pay for the two boundary pixels, so a radius of 6
 at scale 2 costs twenty-four blended pixels for a whole box however tall it is.

  comp-blend-px : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-blend-px (base) (stride) (clip) (x) (y) (c) (a) =
    if a <= 0 then 0
    else if x < clip.lr-x then 0
    else if x >= clip.lr-x + clip.lr-w then 0
    else if y < clip.lr-y then 0
    else if y >= clip.lr-y + clip.lr-h then 0
    else if a >= 255 then gop-put base stride x y c
    else gop-put base stride x y (comp-lerp (gop-get base stride x y) c a 256)

  comp-rows : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, CornerStyle, Integer, Integer, Boolean, Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-rows (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (cs) (c0) (c1) (grad) (horiz) (y) (s) =
    if y >= r.lr-h then 0
    else let ins256 = comp-corner-ins256 cs (r.lr-w) (r.lr-h) y s
    in let aa = ins256 > 0
    in let ip = ins256 / 256
    in let cov = 255 - (ins256 - ip * 256)
    in let ins = if aa then ip + 1 else 0
    in let w = box-clamp0 (r.lr-w - 2 * ins)
    in let x = r.lr-x + ins
    in let flat = if grad then comp-lerp c0 c1 y (r.lr-h) else c0
    in let row = r.lr-y + y
    in let inside = row >= clip.lr-y & row < clip.lr-y + clip.lr-h
    in let one = if w <= 0 then 0 else if inside == False then 0 else if grad & horiz then comp-row-h base stride clip (r.lr-x) (r.lr-w) row x (x + w) c0 c1 else comp-fill base stride clip x row w 1 flat
    in let lx = r.lr-x + ip
    in let rx = r.lr-x + r.lr-w - 1 - ip
    in let lc = if grad & horiz then comp-lerp c0 c1 (lx - r.lr-x) (r.lr-w) else flat
    in let rc = if grad & horiz then comp-lerp c0 c1 (rx - r.lr-x) (r.lr-w) else flat
    in let el = if aa == False then 0 else if inside == False then 0 else comp-blend-px base stride clip lx row lc cov
    in let er = if aa == False then 0 else if inside == False then 0 else if rx <= lx then 0 else comp-blend-px base stride clip rx row rc cov
    in comp-rows base stride clip r cs c0 c1 grad horiz (y + 1) s

  comp-border-box : LayoutRect, WidgetStyle -> LayoutRect
  comp-border-box (b) (st) =
    let m = st.ws-margin
    in let bw = box-clamp0 (b.lr-w - edges-h m)
    in let bh = box-clamp0 (b.lr-h - edges-v m)
    in layout-rect (b.lr-x + m.edge-left) (b.lr-y + m.edge-top) bw bh

  comp-fill-box : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, WidgetStyle, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-fill-box (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (st) (bg) (s) =
    let g = st.ws-gradient
    in let on = g.gr-enabled
    in let c0 = if on then g.gr-start-color else bg
    in let c1 = if on then g.gr-end-color else bg
    in let horiz = on & (g.gr-vertical == False)
    in comp-rows base stride clip r ((st.ws-border).bdr-corner) c0 c1 on horiz 0 s

 `sh-blur` has been carried through `Theme`, the desk's `dk-shade` and the
 Appearance toggle since they were written, and nothing read it: a shadow was
 one offset opaque rectangle. It is a ramp now. Layers are drawn outermost
 first, each one device pixel further out than the last and each at the same
 alpha, so the blends accumulate densely against the box and thin to a single
 layer at the edge.

 The interior is skipped rather than blended and overpainted. `comp-box` fills
 the widget on the next line, so every pixel inside its rectangle is about to
 be replaced; excluding it is what keeps the cost to the visible band instead
 of one full box per layer.

  comp-blend-run : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-blend-run (base) (stride) (clip) (x) (xend) (y) (c) (a) =
    if x >= xend then 0
    else let p = comp-blend-px base stride clip x y c a
    in comp-blend-run base stride clip (x + 1) xend y c a

  comp-shadow-row : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect -> Integer
  comp-shadow-row (base) (stride) (clip) (x0) (x1) (y) (c) (a) (ex) =
    if y < ex.lr-y then comp-blend-run base stride clip x0 x1 y c a
    else if y >= ex.lr-y + ex.lr-h then comp-blend-run base stride clip x0 x1 y c a
    else let l = comp-blend-run base stride clip x0 (math-min x1 (ex.lr-x)) y c a
    in comp-blend-run base stride clip (math-max x0 (ex.lr-x + ex.lr-w)) x1 y c a

  comp-shadow-layer : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, CornerStyle, Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer -> Integer
  comp-shadow-layer (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (cs) (c) (a) (s) (ex) (y) =
    if y >= r.lr-h then 0
    else let ins = comp-corner-ins256 cs (r.lr-w) (r.lr-h) y s / 256
    in let one = comp-shadow-row base stride clip (r.lr-x + ins) (r.lr-x + r.lr-w - ins) (r.lr-y + y) c a ex
    in comp-shadow-layer base stride clip r cs c a s ex (y + 1)

  comp-grow : LayoutRect, Integer -> LayoutRect
  comp-grow (r) (k) = layout-rect (r.lr-x - k) (r.lr-y - k) (r.lr-w + 2 * k) (r.lr-h + 2 * k)

  comp-shadow-stack : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, CornerStyle, Integer, Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer -> Integer
  comp-shadow-stack (base) (stride) (clip) (sr) (cs) (c) (a) (s) (ex) (k) =
    if k < 0 then 0
    else let one = comp-shadow-layer base stride clip (comp-grow sr k) cs c a s ex 0
    in comp-shadow-stack base stride clip sr cs c a s ex (k - 1)

  comp-shadow : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, WidgetStyle, Integer -> Integer
  comp-shadow (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (st) (s) =
    let sh = st.ws-shadow
    in if sh.sh-enabled == False then 0
    else let sr = layout-rect (r.lr-x + sh.sh-offset-x * s) (r.lr-y + sh.sh-offset-y * s) (r.lr-w) (r.lr-h)
    in let cs = (st.ws-border).bdr-corner
    in let k = sh.sh-blur * s
    in if k <= 0 then comp-rows base stride clip sr cs (sh.sh-color) (sh.sh-color) False False 0 s
    else comp-shadow-stack base stride clip sr cs (sh.sh-color) (255 / (k + 1)) s r k

 The sides stop short of the corner radius at each end, which is what makes a
 square outline read as a rounded one without tracing the arc a second time.

  comp-border : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, Border, Integer -> Integer
  comp-border (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (bd) (s) =
    let k = comp-fit-r (bd.bdr-corner) (r.lr-w) (r.lr-h) s
    in let ih = box-clamp0 (r.lr-w - 2 * k)
    in let iv = box-clamp0 (r.lr-h - 2 * k)
    in let tw = (bd.bdr-top).brd-width * s
    in let bw = (bd.bdr-bottom).brd-width * s
    in let lw = (bd.bdr-left).brd-width * s
    in let rw = (bd.bdr-right).brd-width * s
    in let d0 = if tw > 0 & ih > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + k) (r.lr-y) ih tw ((bd.bdr-top).brd-color) else 0
    in let d1 = if bw > 0 & ih > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + k) (r.lr-y + r.lr-h - bw) ih bw ((bd.bdr-bottom).brd-color) else 0
    in let d2 = if lw > 0 & iv > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y + k) lw iv ((bd.bdr-left).brd-color) else 0
    in if rw > 0 & iv > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + r.lr-w - rw) (r.lr-y + k) rw iv ((bd.bdr-right).brd-color) else 0

  comp-accent : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, AccentBorder, Integer -> Integer
  comp-accent (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (ab) (s) =
    if ab.ab-enabled == False then 0
    else let n = ab.ab-width * s
    in if n <= 0 then 0
    else if ab.ab-side == 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y) (r.lr-w) n (ab.ab-color)
    else if ab.ab-side == 1 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + r.lr-w - n) (r.lr-y) n (r.lr-h) (ab.ab-color)
    else if ab.ab-side == 2 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y + r.lr-h - n) (r.lr-w) n (ab.ab-color)
    else comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y) n (r.lr-h) (ab.ab-color)

 The bevel sits INSIDE the border box and stops short of the corner
 radius at each end, exactly as `comp-border` does, so a rounded surface
 keeps its shape instead of growing four square ears. Top and left take
 one colour and bottom and right the other; which is which is
 `bevel-tl` / `bevel-br`, so nothing here has to remember what raised
 means.

  comp-bevel : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, WidgetStyle, Integer -> Integer
  comp-bevel (base) (stride) (clip) (r) (st) (s) =
    let bv = st.ws-bevel
    in if bv.bv-enabled == False then 0
    else let n = bv.bv-width * s
    in if n <= 0 then 0
    else let k = comp-fit-r ((st.ws-border).bdr-corner) (r.lr-w) (r.lr-h) s
    in let ih = box-clamp0 (r.lr-w - 2 * k)
    in let iv = box-clamp0 (r.lr-h - 2 * k)
    in let tl = bevel-tl bv
    in let br = bevel-br bv
    in let d0 = if ih > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + k) (r.lr-y) ih n tl else 0
    in let d1 = if iv > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x) (r.lr-y + k) n iv tl else 0
    in let d2 = if ih > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + k) (r.lr-y + r.lr-h - n) ih n br else 0
    in if iv > 0 then comp-fill base stride clip (r.lr-x + r.lr-w - n) (r.lr-y + k) n iv br else 0

  comp-mul-rect : LayoutRect, Integer -> LayoutRect
  comp-mul-rect (r) (s) = layout-rect (r.lr-x * s) (r.lr-y * s) (r.lr-w * s) (r.lr-h * s)

  comp-box : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, WidgetStyle, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-box (base) (stride) (clip) (b) (st) (bg) (s) =
    let r = comp-mul-rect (comp-border-box b st) s
    in if r.lr-w <= 0 | r.lr-h <= 0 then 0
    else let sh = comp-shadow base stride clip r st s
    in let f = comp-fill-box base stride clip r st bg s
    in let d = comp-border base stride clip r (st.ws-border) s
    in let bv = comp-bevel base stride clip r st s
    in comp-accent base stride clip r (st.ws-accent-border) s

Section: Node Drawing

  comp-glyph-h : Integer = 16

  comp-cell-w : Integer = 8

 Text is all-or-nothing against the clip, and that is a real limitation rather
 than an oversight. `gop-draw-text` paints a glyph as sixteen scaled rows with
 no row bound, so a line straddling the clip cannot be half-drawn without a
 bounded glyph primitive. Dropping it keeps the guarantee this section exists
 for -- nothing drawn here lands outside the clip -- at the cost of a label
 vanishing rather than being cut, which the desk goldens will show if it ever
 happens to one.

 The loaded typeface wins when there is one, which is the same choice
 `desk-line` makes for the desk's hand-drawn text. It was made in one place
 and not the other, so a stick carrying a face rendered its prose
 proportional and every label, button and title beside it as the bitmap
 grid; the widget layer was never handed the face at all.

 The glyph height comes from the FACE and not from `comp-glyph-h * s` on that
 path. The two agree today because `desk-font` loads at exactly that ppem,
 but reading it off the handle keeps this correct if a caller ever loads at
 another size, and centring by the wrong height is the kind of error that
 looks like a layout bug three files away.

 `gbf-put-text` is given the clip's bottom as its buffer height, so the
 TrueType path carries a row bound that `gop-draw-text` does not have. The
 all-or-nothing test above still applies to both.

 CENTRING PUTS THE CAP BAND IN THE MIDDLE, NOT THE CELL. Halving `gf-gh` centres
 the glyph BOUNDING BOX, and a bounding box is not what a reader sees: it spans
 the tallest ascender to the deepest descender, so for cmunss it is 2591 units
 against a 2048 em, 126 per cent. Centring it sat the capitals 8.5 device pixels
 low in a 40 pixel button, measured 2026-08-20, and a label with no descender --
 which is most of them -- looked wrong by that whole amount. The band a reader
 centres on is `gf-asc - gf-cap` to `gf-asc`, so that band is what this centres,
 and the descender is allowed to hang below it the way type does on paper.

 `dy` is often NEGATIVE and that is correct rather than a bug to clamp away: a
 cell taller than its content box must start above it. The clip tests below are
 what keep that safe, and moving the cell UP makes `ty + gh` smaller, so this
 refuses strictly less often than centring the box did.

 The BITMAP path keeps the old arithmetic exactly. `comp-glyph-h` really is the
 cell AND the cap band for that face, so halving it is right there.

 THE PROPORTIONAL PATH MEASURES BEFORE IT DRAWS, which is what lets the
 guarantee this section rests on survive a face whose glyphs are not all one
 width. `comp-render` may clip safely because nothing painted here lands
 outside the clip; with a fixed cell that was arithmetic, and with real
 advances it has to be a measurement. `comp-fit-px` answers the longest prefix
 that fits the box, so the string handed to the primitive is bounded on a
 GLYPH boundary before a pixel is written.

 `gfont-text-clip`'s right edge is still passed and is deliberately not
 removed. It is now a second bound rather than the only one: measuring decides
 what to draw and clipping catches anything the measurement did not (L-FALLBACK
 -- a working path is not disabled in the change that introduces its
 replacement).

 The BITMAP path is unchanged. Its cell really is `comp-cell-w` wide, so
 `comp-cols` and `comp-fit` are correct for it and wrong only for the face.

  comp-text : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, LayoutRect, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-text (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (c) (t) (fg) (s) =
    let gh = if tf.gf-ok then tf.gf-gh else comp-glyph-h * s
    in let dy = if tf.gf-ok then (c.lr-h - tf.gf-cap) / 2 - (tf.gf-asc - tf.gf-cap)
      else if c.lr-h > gh then (c.lr-h - gh) / 2 else 0
    in let ty = c.lr-y + dy
    in if ty < clip.lr-y then 0
    else if ty + gh > clip.lr-y + clip.lr-h then 0
    else if c.lr-x < clip.lr-x then 0
    else if tf.gf-ok then
      let cr = c.lr-x + c.lr-w
      in let pr = clip.lr-x + clip.lr-w
      in let right = if cr < pr then cr else pr
      in let shown = comp-fit-px tf t (right - c.lr-x)
      in gfont-text-clip tf base stride (clip.lr-y + clip.lr-h) right (c.lr-x) ty shown fg
    else gop-draw-text base stride font (c.lr-x) ty (comp-fit t (comp-cols c s)) fg s 0

 THE CALLER NO LONGER DECIDES WHAT FITS. Every call site used to hand this
 `comp-fit t (comp-cols c s)`, chopping the string to `box width / 8s`
 CHARACTERS before the face was consulted, so a narrow string was cut early on
 the proportional path whatever `comp-fit-px` then measured. The bitmap branch
 keeps that arithmetic exactly, because for a cell that really is
 `comp-cell-w` wide it is correct. One function now answers "what fits", which
 is what lets it be the same answer the draw is bounded by.

 The whole string is measured first and returned untouched when it fits, which
 is the common case and costs one walk of the advance table and no allocation.
 Only an overflow searches, and it searches by halving rather than stepping so
 the substrings it builds are bounded by log2 of the length: a 32-character
 label that does not fit allocates 5, not 32. A step-by-one search reads more
 simply and allocates one string per character on every repaint, into an arena
 with no collector, which is the shape R-COST exists to catch.

  comp-fit-px : GopFont, Text, Integer -> Text
  comp-fit-px (tf) (t) (avail) =
    if avail <= 0 then ""
    else if gfont-text-w tf t <= avail then t
    else comp-fit-px-loop tf t avail 0 (text-length t - 1)

  comp-fit-px-loop : GopFont, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  comp-fit-px-loop (tf) (t) (avail) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then substring t 0 lo
    else let mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2
    in if gfont-text-w tf (substring t 0 mid) <= avail
      then comp-fit-px-loop tf t avail mid hi
      else comp-fit-px-loop tf t avail lo (mid - 1)

  comp-cols : LayoutRect, Integer -> Integer
  comp-cols (c) (s) = c.lr-w / (comp-cell-w * s)

  comp-gauge-fill : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-gauge-fill (base) (stride) (clip) (b) (v) (mx) (fg) =
    if mx <= 0 then 0
    else let fw = (b.lr-w) * (if v > mx then mx else v) / mx
    in if fw <= 0 then 0
    else comp-fill base stride clip (b.lr-x) (b.lr-y) fw (b.lr-h) fg

  comp-fit : Text, Integer -> Text
  comp-fit (t) (cols) =
    if cols <= 0 then ""
    else if text-length t <= cols then t
    else substring t 0 cols

 THE CARET IS PLACED BY THE SAME METRIC THAT DREW THE TEXT, which is the only
 way it lands between the right two glyphs. Its x is the measured width of the
 first `cursor` characters, not `cursor` times an assumed cell: on a
 proportional face those disagree by more with every character typed, so a
 caret set by multiplication drifts away from the text it is supposed to sit
 in. Its height and its top are the CAP BAND -- `gf-cap` at
 `(c.lr-h - gf-cap) / 2` -- which is exactly where `comp-text` puts the band,
 so the two cannot come apart.

 The bitmap branch keeps the multiplication and `comp-glyph-h`, because for
 that face the cell really is `comp-cell-w` wide and the two agree.

  comp-input : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, WidgetStyle, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-input (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (b) (c) (st) (t) (cursor) (fg) (s) =
    let fill = comp-box base stride clip b st (st.ws-bg) s
    in let txt = comp-text base stride clip font tf c t fg s
    in let n = if cursor < 0 then 0 else if cursor > text-length t then text-length t else cursor
    in let gh = if tf.gf-ok then tf.gf-cap else comp-glyph-h * s
    in let want = if tf.gf-ok then c.lr-x + gfont-text-w tf (substring t 0 n)
      else c.lr-x + cursor * comp-cell-w * s
    in let hi = c.lr-x + c.lr-w - 1
    in let cx = if want > hi then hi else want
    in let ch = if c.lr-h < gh then c.lr-h else gh
    in let dy = if c.lr-h > gh then (c.lr-h - gh) / 2 else 0
    in if ch <= 0 | cx < c.lr-x then 0
    else comp-fill base stride clip cx (c.lr-y + dy) s ch fg

 `StatusBadge` has three custom tags and this chapter answered none of them,
 so every one fell to the box branch below. `status-dot` and
 `status-dot-filled` painted the SAME rectangle, which is the whole content
 of the distinction between them, and `BsIcon` did not reach here at all
 because it rendered as a label carrying the icon's NAME as text.

 A dot is a rounded rectangle whose radius is half its side, which
 `comp-rows` and `comp-corner-ins256` already draw; there is no circle
 primitive here and this needs none. The hollow one is the same shape
 painted again inset in the background colour, which is a ring without a
 second path to maintain. The rect handed to `comp-rows` is already a DEVICE
 rectangle, so the scale it is given is 1: the radius must not be multiplied
 a second time.

 These take the node's BORDER box and not its content box, and the size in
 the badge is why. `badge-widget` emits its dots at 10 by 10 logical, and at
 that size the style's padding leaves a content box with nothing in it, so a
 dot measured off the content box drew ZERO pixels while a 20 by 20 one
 drew fine. Padding exists to inset TEXT from an edge; a picture that fills
 its node has nothing to inset, and the node's box is the picture.

  comp-dot : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  comp-dot (base) (stride) (clip) (c) (fg) (bg) (filled) =
    let d = math-min (c.lr-w) (c.lr-h)
    in if d <= 0 then 0
    else let x = c.lr-x + (c.lr-w - d) / 2
    in let y = c.lr-y + (c.lr-h - d) / 2
    in let outer = comp-rows base stride clip (layout-rect x y d d) (CornerRound (d / 2)) fg fg False False 0 1
    in if filled then outer
    else let t = if d >= 6 then d / 4 else 1
    in let id = d - 2 * t
    in if id <= 0 then outer
    else comp-rows base stride clip (layout-rect (x + t) (y + t) id id) (CornerRound (id / 2)) bg bg False False 0 1

 An icon is all-or-nothing against the clip, exactly as text is and for the
 same reason: `gicon-blit` bounds its columns against the stride but knows
 nothing of a clip rectangle, so a partly visible icon cannot be half drawn
 without a second bounded primitive. Dropping it keeps the guarantee this
 section exists for.

 The icon scales to the box it is given rather than to the layout scale,
 because a badge's box is 16 by 16 LOGICAL and the artwork is 8 wide: the
 scale is the room divided by the artwork, and a box under 8 device pixels
 still draws at 1 rather than vanishing.

  comp-icon-tag : Text -> Boolean
  comp-icon-tag (tag) =
    if text-length tag <= 5 then False else substring tag 0 5 == "icon:"

  comp-icon : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, Text, Integer -> Integer
  comp-icon (base) (stride) (clip) (c) (tag) (fg) =
    let name = substring tag 5 (text-length tag - 5)
    in let d = math-min (c.lr-w) (c.lr-h)
    in let sc = if d < 8 then 1 else d / 8
    in let sz = 8 * sc
    in let x = c.lr-x + (c.lr-w - sz) / 2
    in let y = c.lr-y + (c.lr-h - sz) / 2
    in if x < clip.lr-x then 0
    else if y < clip.lr-y then 0
    else if x + sz > clip.lr-x + clip.lr-w then 0
    else if y + sz > clip.lr-y + clip.lr-h then 0
    else gicon-blit base stride (gicon-named name) x y sc fg

  comp-custom : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, LayoutRect, WidgetStyle, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-custom (base) (stride) (clip) (b) (c) (st) (tag) (fg) (s) =
    if tag == wk-scroll-view-tag then 0
    else if tag == "event-dot"
    then comp-fill base stride clip (c.lr-x + c.lr-w / 2 - 3 * s) (c.lr-y + c.lr-h / 2 - 3 * s) (6 * s) (6 * s) fg
    else if tag == "status-dot" then comp-dot base stride clip (comp-mul-rect b s) fg (st.ws-bg) False
    else if tag == "status-dot-filled" then comp-dot base stride clip (comp-mul-rect b s) fg (st.ws-bg) True
    else if comp-icon-tag tag then comp-icon base stride clip (comp-mul-rect b s) tag fg
    else comp-box base stride clip b st (st.ws-bg) s

 A node's TONE overrides the style's foreground: `tone-success` paints a
 label green wherever the theme would have painted it plain. It is how
 `StatusBadge` and everything built on it says what it means, and this
 chapter ignored it -- so a badge rendered here was the same colour
 whatever it was reporting, which is the whole content of a badge.
 `theme-tone-fg` falls through to the style's own foreground for
 `tone-none`, so this is not a special case, it is the general one.

  comp-fg : Theme, WidgetNode, WidgetStyle -> Integer
  comp-fg (th) (node) (st) = theme-tone-fg th (node.wn-tone) (st.ws-fg)

  comp-draw-node : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer -> Integer
  comp-draw-node (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (node) (th) (s) =
    let st = widget-resolve-style node th
    in let b = node.wn-bounds
    in let c = comp-mul-rect (box-content-from-style b st) s
    in let fg = comp-fg th node st
    in when node.wn-kind
      is WkPanel -> comp-box base stride clip b st (st.ws-bg) s
      is WkSeparator -> comp-box base stride clip b st (st.ws-fg) s
      is WkLabel (t) -> comp-text base stride clip font tf c t fg s
      is WkButton (t) ->
        let bg = comp-box base stride clip b st (st.ws-bg) s
        in comp-text base stride clip font tf c t fg s
      is WkGauge (v) (mx) ->
        let bg = comp-box base stride clip b st (st.ws-bg) s
        in comp-gauge-fill base stride clip c v mx fg
      is WkInput (t) (cur) -> comp-input base stride clip font tf b c st t cur fg s
      is WkCustom (tag) -> comp-custom base stride clip b c st tag fg s
      is otherwise -> 0

Section: Translate

 Moving a laid-out subtree is what scrolling a pane of arbitrary content is,
 and it is only safe now that the walk clips: content pushed out of the box
 used to be painted anyway, over whatever was next and past the end of the
 framebuffer.

 `scroll-slice` answers the other shape, a list of uniform rows windowed by
 index, and a page is labels and separators of differing heights with no row
 height to give it.

 The bounds on a laid tree are LOGICAL, so the shift is logical too: a caller
 holding a device offset divides by the same scale it renders at.

 This stores in place rather than rebuilding the way `widget-set-bounds` does,
 and the reason is that a rebuild would have to rebuild every ancestor as well
 to repoint their children. `widget-arrange` hands back a tree whose nodes and
 child lists are freshly allocated and shared with nothing, and this runs on
 that tree between the layout and the walk, so the only cost is one rectangle
 per node instead of a second copy of the subtree.

  comp-translate : WidgetNode, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  comp-translate (n) (dx) (dy) =
    let b = n.wn-bounds
    in let moved = __record-set n "wn-bounds" (layout-rect (b.lr-x + dx) (b.lr-y + dy) (b.lr-w) (b.lr-h))
    in let k = comp-translate-kids (n.wn-children) dx dy 0
    in moved

  comp-translate-kids : List WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-translate-kids (ks) (dx) (dy) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ks then 0
    else let t = comp-translate (list-at ks i) dx dy
    in comp-translate-kids ks dx dy (i + 1)

Section: Tree Walk

 The clip does not narrow as the walk descends. A child painting outside its
 own parent is the defect this bounds, and narrowing per node would hide it
 rather than surface it: the tree still lays out wrong and the goldens would
 stop being able to say so. The bound is the box the render was given, once.

  comp-walk : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer -> Integer
  comp-walk (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (node) (th) (s) =
    let d = comp-draw-node base stride clip font tf node th s
    in comp-walk-kids base stride clip font tf (node.wn-children) th 0 s

  comp-walk-kids : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, List WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-walk-kids (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (kids) (th) (i) (s) =
    if i >= list-length kids then 0
    else let w = comp-walk base stride clip font tf (list-at kids i) th s
    in comp-walk-kids base stride clip font tf kids th (i + 1) s

 A scrolled subtree is painted by a SECOND walk under a tighter clip, so the
 first walk has to leave it alone. Skipping is by id because a node is a record
 and there is no identity comparison to ask with.

  comp-walk-except : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Text -> Integer
  comp-walk-except (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (node) (th) (s) (skip) =
    if node.wn-id == skip then 0
    else let d = comp-draw-node base stride clip font tf node th s
    in comp-walk-except-kids base stride clip font tf (node.wn-children) th 0 s skip

  comp-walk-except-kids : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, CbfFont, GopFont, List WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Integer, Text -> Integer
  comp-walk-except-kids (base) (stride) (clip) (font) (tf) (kids) (th) (i) (s) (skip) =
    if i >= list-length kids then 0
    else let w = comp-walk-except base stride clip font tf (list-at kids i) th s skip
    in comp-walk-except-kids base stride clip font tf kids th (i + 1) s skip

  comp-clip : LayoutRect, LayoutRect -> LayoutRect
  comp-clip (a) (b) =
    let x0 = math-max (a.lr-x) (b.lr-x)
    in let y0 = math-max (a.lr-y) (b.lr-y)
    in let x1 = math-min (a.lr-x + a.lr-w) (b.lr-x + b.lr-w)
    in let y1 = math-min (a.lr-y + a.lr-h) (b.lr-y + b.lr-h)
    in layout-rect x0 y0 (box-clamp0 (x1 - x0)) (box-clamp0 (y1 - y0))

Section: Render

 THE TREE IS LAID OUT IN LOGICAL PIXELS AND PAINTED IN DEVICE PIXELS. The
 layout runs against `w / s` by `h / s`, so a theme's 4-pixel padding, a
 160-pixel sidebar and every app's own minimum sizes are written once and
 come out the same physical size at any resolution. Only this chapter
 multiplies, which is why nothing else had to learn about scale.

 The bounds on the returned tree are therefore LOGICAL. A hit test against
 pointer coordinates, which are device, must divide them by the same `s`;
 `comp-hit-x` and `comp-hit-y` are here so no caller invents that itself.

  comp-hit-x : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-hit-x (mx) (s) = mx / s

  comp-hit-y : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  comp-hit-y (my) (s) = my / s

 The clip is the device box the caller asked for, so `comp-render` keeps its
 signature and every existing caller gains the bound without knowing it. A
 caller that hands `w` and `h` smaller than the framebuffer, which is what the
 desk does for a pane, is bounded to the pane and not merely to the screen.

  comp-fit-w : GopFont, Integer, Text -> Integer
  comp-fit-w (tf) (s) (t) = (gfont-text-w tf t + s - 1) / s

 A caller that pinned a width with `widget-set-min` MEANT that number, and it
 lands in the same field as the eight pixel cell `widget-label` guessed. The
 taskbar has one of each: the menu button is pinned to 72 and the clock label
 to 220 over an EMPTY string, which a face-measured width would collapse to
 nothing. So a cell is replaced only while it still holds the guess.

  comp-fit-guess : WidgetNode, Integer -> Boolean
  comp-fit-guess (n) (want) = n.wn-min-w == want

  comp-fit-node : WidgetNode, GopFont, Integer -> WidgetNode
  comp-fit-node (n) (tf) (s) =
    let fitted = when n.wn-kind
      is WkLabel (t) -> if comp-fit-guess n (text-length t * 8)
        then widget-set-min n (comp-fit-w tf s t) (n.wn-min-h) else n
      is WkButton (t) -> if comp-fit-guess n (text-length t * 8 + 16)
        then widget-set-min n (comp-fit-w tf s t + 16) (n.wn-min-h) else n
      is otherwise -> n
    in if fitted.wn-child-count == 0 then fitted
    else widget-set-children fitted (comp-fit-kids (fitted.wn-children) tf s 0 (fitted.wn-child-count) [])

  comp-fit-kids : List WidgetNode, GopFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  comp-fit-kids (kids) (tf) (s) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else comp-fit-kids kids tf s (i + 1) n (list-push acc (comp-fit-node (list-at kids i) tf s))

  comp-fit-text : WidgetNode, GopFont, Integer -> WidgetNode
  comp-fit-text (root) (tf) (s) = if tf.gf-ok then comp-fit-node root tf s else root

  comp-render : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, GopFont, WidgetNode, Theme, Integer, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  comp-render (base) (stride) (font) (tf) (root) (th) (w) (h) (s) =
    let laid = widget-layout root th (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))
    in let d = comp-walk base stride (layout-rect 0 0 w h) font tf laid th s
    in laid
